{"DDInter1033":{"severity":"Major","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"The recent, concomitant, or subsequent use (without the recommended leflunomide washout period or procedure) of other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury associated with leflunomide. The risk is thought to extend to teriflunomide, its principal active metabolite, because recommended dosages of both result in a similar range of plasma concentrations of teriflunomide.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1046":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1085":{"severity":"Major","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration of lomitapide with other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury. Lomitapide can cause elevations in serum transaminases and hepatic steatosis. Clinical data suggest that hepatic fat accumulation is reversible after stopping treatment with lomitapide, although the long-term consequences are unknown.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1197":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter12":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1228":{"severity":"Major","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration of mipomersen with other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury. Mipomersen can cause elevations in serum transaminases and hepatic steatosis.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter126":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter129":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1291":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1301":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1313":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1325":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter133":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter134":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1348":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Orlistat may theoretically reduce the gastrointestinal absorption and therapeutic efficacy of antiretroviral agents. The mechanism may involve retention of lipophilic medicines in the gastrointestinal tract or reduced gastrointestinal tract transit time.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1365":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1409":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1435":{"severity":"Major","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Serious cases of hepatotoxicity, some fatal, have occurred in patients treated with pexidartinib. Concomitant use of other potentially hepatotoxic agents may potentiate the risk of liver injury. The mechanism of hepatotoxicity is unknown, its occurrence cannot be predicted, and it is unknown whether liver injury occurs in the absence of increased transaminases.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1472":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1494":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1533":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1553":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1559":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1578":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Concomitant use of remdesivir with other agents that are known to induce hepatotoxicity may theoretically increase the risk of liver injury. Data from investigational use and clinical studies suggest that remdesivir may be associated with hepatotoxic effects.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1600":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration with inhibitors of CYP450 1A1 may theoretically increase the plasma concentrations of riociguat, which is partially metabolized by the isoenzyme to its main active metabolite (1/10th to 1/3rd the pharmacologic activity of riociguat). Increased levels of riociguat may increase the risk for hypotension.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1622":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1640":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1662":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter170":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1771":{"severity":"Major","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"The recent, concomitant, or subsequent use (without the recommended leflunomide washout period or procedure) of other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury associated with leflunomide. The risk is thought to extend to teriflunomide, its principal active metabolite, because recommended dosages of both result in a similar range of plasma concentrations of teriflunomide.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1840":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration of trabectedin with other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter1906":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1912":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter228":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter246":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter252":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter262":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter264":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter284":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration of cannabidiol with other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury. Cannabidiol causes dose-related elevations of liver transaminases, both alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST).","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter305":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter349":{"severity":"Minor","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Administration of abacavir with other agents known to use the glucuronyl transferase or alcohol dehydrogenase metabolic pathways may increase the area under the time concentration curve for both drugs. The clinical significance is unknown.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter393":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter398":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter402":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter411":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter424":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Cobicistat may increase the plasma concentrations of antiretroviral agents. The plasma concentrations of cobicistat may also be increased or reduced in the presence of antiretroviral agents. The proposed mechanism is cobicistat inhibition of the CYP450 3A4 isoenzyme, of which antiretroviral agents may be substrates, and the inhibition or induction of CYP450 3A4 by concomitant antiretroviral medications.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter513":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter593":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter677":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter766":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter777":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter801":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter812":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter826":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter83":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter856":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter900":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter905":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Coadministration of idelalisib with other agents known to induce hepatotoxicity may potentiate the risk of liver injury. The use of idelalisib has been associated with elevations in alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) greater than 5 times the upper limit of normal.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter995":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1012":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter1018":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter